      en: `Work on Lalbagh Fort began in 1678 under Prince Muhammad Azam, a son of the emperor Aurangzeb. The governor Shaista Khan carried it on, but after the early death of his beloved daughter Pari Bibi the project was considered ill-omened and abandoned, which is why the fort survives as an unfinished Mughal dream.\n\nThree main buildings stand on a single axis inside the walls: a three-domed mosque to the west, the tomb of Pari Bibi in the centre and, to the east, the Diwan-i-Aam audience hall with its bathhouse, now a small museum of Mughal-era objects. Straight paths, water channels and fountains between them keep the rhythm of a classic Mughal garden.\n\nThe fort is at its best on a winter morning, when sunlight cuts through the mist onto the red brick and the lawns are quiet. By late afternoon families and students fill the grounds, and the garden becomes a welcome green pause in the noise of Old Dhaka.`,
