      en: `Nawab Abdul Ghani of Dhaka built this palace on the Buriganga in the nineteenth century and named it after his son, Khwaja Ahsanullah. A violent tornado in 1888 badly damaged the building; it was rebuilt, and the reconstruction gave it the tall central dome and the pink colour it is known for today.\n\nFor decades Ahsan Manzil was the seat of the Nawab family, their court, guesthouse and a meeting place for the politics of eastern Bengal. After restoration it became a museum run under the National Museum, and its many rooms now display the family's furniture, portraits, weapons and objects of daily life, with some halls arranged as they might once have looked.\n\nA grand staircase climbs from the riverside lawn to a wide veranda where you can watch the constant traffic of boats and launches on the Buriganga. Winter mornings are the calmest time to visit, while late-afternoon light makes the pink facade glow.`,
